abstract="We introduce tatami tilings, and present some of the many interesting questions that arise when studying them. Roughly speaking, we are considering tilings of rectilinear regions with 1 {\texttimes}2 dimer tiles and 1 {\texttimes}1 monomer tiles, with the constraint that no four corners of the tiles meet. Typical problems are to minimize the number of monomers in a tiling, or to count the number of tilings in a particular shape. We determine the underlying structure of tatami tilings of rectangles and use this to prove that the number of tatami tilings of an n {\texttimes}n square with n monomers is n2n{\thinspace}−{\thinspace}1. We also prove that, for fixed-height, the number of tatami tilings of a rectangle is a rational function and outline an algorithm that produces the coefficients of the two polynomials of the numerator and the denominator.",
isbn="978-3-642-14031-0"
}

